4
00:00:12,587 --> 00:00:15,010
{3}His daughter Maya grabs
her phone and dials 911.

5
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:20,454
{4}And from that moment, a whole chain
of people, most of whom Frank will

6
00:00:20,504 --> 00:00:25,958
never meet, starts working on
one job: keeping him alive.

7
00:00:26,008 --> 00:00:31,008
{5}The caller, the dispatcher, the
ambulance crew, and the hospital.

8
00:00:31,508 --> 00:00:37,325
That chain is emergency medical
services, the EMS system, and by the end

9
00:00:37,375 --> 00:00:41,478
of this video you'll be able to follow
Frank's call through every link of

10
00:00:41,528 --> 00:00:46,050
it. {1}You'll know where that system
came from, {2}the federal laws and

11
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:51,114
the ten standards that built it, {3}how
a 911 call actually finds a caller,

12
00:00:51,164 --> 00:00:55,805
{4}and why the ambulance doesn't
always go to the closest hospital.

13
00:00:55,855 --> 00:01:01,658
{5}And all of that is chapter one material
for your EMT course and the national

14
00:01:01,708 --> 00:01:02,588
exam, the NREMT.

15
00:01:03,088 --> 00:01:07,715
Now, chapter one usually feels like a
pile of trivia, so most students just

16
00:01:07,765 --> 00:01:10,783
memorize dates and lists
and hope for the best.

17
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:14,383
{1}But there's one idea that
makes all of it hang together.

18
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:18,563
{2}The whole point of EMS is that
real care starts at the scene, {3}and

19
00:01:18,613 --> 00:01:24,545
then it never stops, through every
handoff, all the way into the hospital.

20
00:01:24,595 --> 00:01:28,963
And as you'll see, that
scene starts with the caller.

21
00:01:29,013 --> 00:01:33,803
{4}Every date and every standard you're
about to see is just a piece that

22
00:01:33,853 --> 00:01:36,403
was added to make that chain work.

23
00:01:36,453 --> 00:01:38,333
So let's watch it get built.

24
00:01:41,383 --> 00:01:42,150
So where did this start?

25
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:46,673
{1}Organized battlefield ambulances show
up in France in the seventeen nineties,

26
00:01:46,723 --> 00:01:51,240
{2}and their main job was to get
wounded soldiers off the battlefield.

27
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:53,423
{3}There was very little
care along the way.

28
00:01:53,473 --> 00:01:55,903
{4}The idea was simply to
get them to the surgeons.

29
00:01:56,403 --> 00:02:00,777
Then, during the American Civil War,
{1}Clara Barton organized care for

30
00:02:00,827 --> 00:02:04,764
wounded soldiers, and she went on
to found the American Red Cross.

31
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:08,977
{2}By the First World War, motorized
ambulances and volunteer ambulance

32
00:02:09,027 --> 00:02:10,657
corps were in wide use.

33
00:02:10,707 --> 00:02:14,697
{3}And the wars in Korea and
Vietnam brought the big advances.

34
00:02:14,747 --> 00:02:19,089
Medics treated wounded soldiers right
where they fell, and helicopters flew

35
00:02:19,139 --> 00:02:20,449
them straight to surgery.

36
00:02:20,499 --> 00:02:24,630
{4}That model, care on the spot and
then a fast trip to a surgeon who's

37
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,964
ready, is what civilian
trauma centers later copied.

38
00:02:28,014 --> 00:02:29,867
We'll meet them in a few minutes.

39
00:02:30,367 --> 00:02:34,713
Meanwhile, back home, civilians
were a long way behind the military.

40
00:02:34,763 --> 00:02:39,017
{1}Some cities had hospital ambulances
by the late eighteen hundreds, but

41
00:02:39,067 --> 00:02:40,590
care on board was minimal.

42
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,637
It was mostly a ride.

43
00:02:42,687 --> 00:02:47,118
{2}And many smaller communities
had no real service for decades.

44
00:02:47,168 --> 00:02:50,842
{3}Well into the nineteen sixties,
the ambulance in a lot of towns was a

45
00:02:50,892 --> 00:02:55,075
funeral home's hearse, because that
was the one vehicle in town built to

46
00:02:55,125 --> 00:02:58,952
carry a person lying flat, {4}or
it was the fire department.

47
00:02:59,452 --> 00:03:02,335
So here's the shift that
defines the history of EMS.

48
00:03:02,385 --> 00:03:04,487
{1}It went from just transport...

49
00:03:04,537 --> 00:03:09,111
{2}to care of hospital quality that
begins at the scene {3}and continues

50
00:03:09,161 --> 00:03:12,695
without a break until the
patient reaches the hospital.

51
00:03:12,745 --> 00:03:14,642
{4}And think about why
that matters for Frank.

52
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:20,035
If his heart stops at that dinner
table, no blood reaches his brain, and

53
00:03:20,085 --> 00:03:22,975
the brain starts being
damaged within minutes.

54
00:03:23,025 --> 00:03:27,355
{5}So a fast ride to a hospital that's
fifteen minutes away won't save him

55
00:03:27,405 --> 00:03:33,402
on its own. He needs help in the
first few minutes, right where he is.

56
00:03:33,452 --> 00:03:35,865
Everything else in your EMT
course rests on that idea.

57
00:03:36,365 --> 00:03:41,274
Quick check. {1}What was the one thing
the earliest EMS services did, and

58
00:03:41,324 --> 00:03:42,292
what were they missing?

59
00:03:46,792 --> 00:03:50,310
They transported the wounded,
and that's all they did.

60
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:53,210
{1}What was missing was care at the scene.

61
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:57,723
{2}And adding that care, then keeping
it going all the way to the hospital,

62
00:03:57,773 --> 00:04:01,555
is what turned an ambulance from a
ride into the start of treatment.

63
00:04:04,605 --> 00:04:08,355
So how did the United States
go from hearses to real EMS?

64
00:04:08,405 --> 00:04:13,168
{1}It took a few federal actions, and
the best way to remember them is to

65
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:15,366
ask what problem each one solved.

66
00:04:15,416 --> 00:04:20,306
{2}In the nineteen sixties, more and more
people were dying on the new highways,

67
00:04:20,356 --> 00:04:24,555
{3}and a nineteen sixty six report
from the National Academy of Sciences,

68
00:04:24,605 --> 00:04:30,395
called Accidental Death and Disability,
described injury as the neglected

69
00:04:30,445 --> 00:04:35,445
disease of modern society, because
care after a crash was so poor.

70
00:04:35,945 --> 00:04:40,112
And since highway crashes were a
transportation problem, the government

71
00:04:40,162 --> 00:04:43,239
handed the job to transportation.

72
00:04:43,289 --> 00:04:48,679
{1}That same year, nineteen sixty six,
the National Highway Safety Act directed

73
00:04:48,729 --> 00:04:53,095
the Department of Transportation,
the DOT, to set standards for EMS.

74
00:04:53,145 --> 00:04:58,865
{2}That's why, even today, the agency
