101
00:06:50,655 --> 00:06:52,475
trained. That's you.

102
00:06:52,975 --> 00:06:56,417
The second group is getting Frank to care.

103
00:06:56,467 --> 00:06:59,905
{1}Transportation is
safe, reliable ambulances.

104
00:06:59,955 --> 00:07:04,985
{2}Facilities means getting each patient
to the right hospital, which isn't

105
00:07:05,035 --> 00:07:06,265
always the closest one.

106
00:07:06,315 --> 00:07:11,945
{3}Communications is the link that
makes it all work, from Maya's call to

107
00:07:11,995 --> 00:07:14,905
the radio report the
crew gives the hospital.

108
00:07:14,955 --> 00:07:19,225
{4}And trauma systems are the networks
of trauma centers that injured patients

109
00:07:19,275 --> 00:07:20,155
are sent to.

110
00:07:20,655 --> 00:07:25,118
The third group keeps
the whole system honest.

111
00:07:25,168 --> 00:07:29,881
{1}Public information and education is
teaching people to recognize an emergency

112
00:07:29,931 --> 00:07:34,889
and call 911, and that's
exactly what Maya just did.

113
00:07:34,939 --> 00:07:39,625
{2}Medical direction means a physician
oversees the care EMTs give, because

114
00:07:39,675 --> 00:07:42,089
EMTs do medical care under
a doctor's authority.

115
00:07:42,139 --> 00:07:47,425
{3}And evaluation means checking the
work and improving it, through quality

116
00:07:47,475 --> 00:07:51,745
improvement, quality assurance and
total quality management, which you'll

117
00:07:51,795 --> 00:07:54,905
see written as QI, QA and TQM.

118
00:07:54,955 --> 00:07:58,838
For now, you just need
to recognize those names.

119
00:07:58,888 --> 00:08:02,985
{4}We'll dig into medical direction
and quality improvement in the next

120
00:08:03,035 --> 00:08:03,835
video.

121
00:08:04,055 --> 00:08:09,567
Quick check. {1}Every month, a system
reviews its run reports, the written

122
00:08:09,617 --> 00:08:14,038
record a crew fills out for each
call, to find problems in patient care

123
00:08:14,088 --> 00:08:17,428
and fix them. Which of
the ten standards is that?

124
00:08:21,928 --> 00:08:26,030
That's evaluation, through
quality improvement.

125
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:28,142
{1}The trap answer is medical direction.

126
00:08:28,192 --> 00:08:33,734
A physician might be involved in the
review, but finding problems and fixing

127
00:08:33,784 --> 00:08:35,760
them is what evaluation means.

128
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:41,606
Okay, so back to Maya, who's on the phone.

129
00:08:41,656 --> 00:08:44,488
{1}The public reaches EMS by
telephone, and the number is 911.

130
00:08:44,538 --> 00:08:49,107
{2}Nine one one is available to nearly
everyone in the country, {3}it handles

131
00:08:49,157 --> 00:08:53,674
about two hundred forty million calls
a year, {4}and in many areas, eighty

132
00:08:53,724 --> 00:08:57,400
percent or more of those
calls come from mobile phones.

133
00:08:57,450 --> 00:09:00,214
And that last number
creates a real problem.

134
00:09:00,264 --> 00:09:02,211
Where is the caller?

135
00:09:02,711 --> 00:09:05,258
With an old landline, that's easy.

136
00:09:05,308 --> 00:09:09,505
{1}Enhanced 911 automatically shows
the dispatcher the caller's number and

137
00:09:09,555 --> 00:09:13,365
the address, because a landline
is wired to one fixed address.

138
00:09:13,415 --> 00:09:17,845
{2}So even if the caller can't speak,
the dispatcher knows where to send

139
00:09:17,895 --> 00:09:18,695
help.

140
00:09:18,835 --> 00:09:23,596
A cell phone moves around, so wireless
911 is rolled out in two phases.

141
00:09:23,646 --> 00:09:28,127
{1}Phase one gives the dispatcher the
callback number, plus the location

142
00:09:28,177 --> 00:09:30,369
of the cell tower that's
handling the call.

143
00:09:30,419 --> 00:09:34,230
{2}But a tower can cover miles,
so that's only a rough area.

144
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:39,483
{3}Phase two adds the actual location of
the device itself, as map coordinates.

145
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:44,470
{4}So if Maya's call drops, phase
one means the dispatcher can call her

146
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:47,590
back, and phase two means
they can find the house.

147
00:09:48,070 --> 00:09:51,100
Calls placed over the
internet are even harder.

148
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:54,931
{1}They're called VoIP calls, for voice
over internet protocol, {2}and some

149
00:09:54,981 --> 00:09:59,473
of them aren't tied to a phone line
or a cell tower, so the location they

150
00:09:59,523 --> 00:10:03,740
send may be just an address the user
registered, which can be out of date.

151
00:10:03,790 --> 00:10:08,150
{3}So locating some of these callers
is still an ongoing challenge.

152
00:10:08,650 --> 00:10:13,334
Quick check. {1}A caller on a cell
phone panics and hangs up before giving

153
00:10:13,384 --> 00:10:17,600
an address. Which wireless phase lets
the dispatcher see where the phone

154
00:10:17,650 --> 00:10:18,677
actually is?

155
00:10:23,177 --> 00:10:27,647
Phase two. {1}It gives the
location of the device itself.

156
00:10:27,697 --> 00:10:31,980
{2}Phase one gives the callback number
and only the tower's location, which

157
00:10:32,030 --> 00:10:36,807
is a rough area. So phase one lets
them call back, and phase two lets them

158
00:10:36,857 --> 00:10:37,857
find the caller.

159
00:10:40,907 --> 00:10:45,857
Now, who answers? {1}It's an emergency
medical dispatcher, an EMD, and EMDs

160
00:10:45,907 --> 00:10:47,401
are specially trained and certified.

161
00:10:47,451 --> 00:10:52,009
{2}The EMD asks a set of questions to
figure out what's going on, and then

162
00:10:52,059 --> 00:10:52,859
sends the right resources.

163
00:10:52,899 --> 00:10:57,657
{3}So for Frank, with chest pain, that
could mean an ambulance with lights

164
00:10:57,707 --> 00:11:01,907
and siren. {4}But the EMD's job
doesn't end when the ambulance leaves.

165
00:11:02,407 --> 00:11:07,370
Because right then, Frank slumps sideways
in his chair, and he's not breathing

166
00:11:07,420 --> 00:11:11,509
normally. {1}The dispatcher tells
Maya to put the phone on speaker, get

167
00:11:11,559 --> 00:11:16,309
him onto the floor, because compressions
work best against a firm surface,

168
00:11:16,359 --> 00:11:20,177
and push hard and fast in
the center of his chest.

169
00:11:20,227 --> 00:11:22,217
{2}Those are pre-arrival instructions.

170
00:11:22,267 --> 00:11:25,500
They're what the EMD tells the caller
to do until the crew gets there.

171
00:11:25,550 --> 00:11:29,629
{3}Things like how to do CPR, which
means chest compressions, sometimes

172
00:11:29,679 --> 00:11:34,287
with rescue breaths, {4}how to give
breaths, {5}or how to control bleeding.

173
00:11:34,787 --> 00:11:38,177
And this is where the big idea
from the start shows up again.

174
00:11:38,227 --> 00:11:42,977
{1}The ambulance is still minutes
away, {2}but care has already started,

175
00:11:43,027 --> 00:11:47,877
at the scene, done by a family
member and guided by the dispatcher.

176
00:11:47,927 --> 00:11:50,630
{3}So the chain doesn't
start when the EMTs walk in.

177
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:52,387
It starts with the caller.

178
00:11:55,437 --> 00:11:59,247
So let's follow Frank through the system,
from his point of view, {1}because

179
00:11:59,297 --> 00:12:03,227
seen from the patient's side,
EMS is a chain of handoffs.

180
00:12:03,277 --> 00:12:06,307
{2}The EMD answered the
call and sent responders.

181
00:12:06,357 --> 00:12:10,827
{3}Now, depending on the system and
the call, those responders can be at

182
00:12:10,877 --> 00:12:16,240
four levels. {4}Emergency medical
responders, or EMRs, {5}emergency medical

183
00:12:16,290 --> 00:12:20,467
technicians, which is the level you're
training for, {6}advanced EMTs, or

184
00:12:20,517 --> 00:12:21,854
